## WSL container

# building and running Linux containers on Windows using wslc.exe, the container CLI that ships built in with WSL.

# wslc.exe provides a familiar CLI for building, shipping, and running containerized apps.

# wslc.exe is included with WSL, so there's no separate engine to install. It requires WSL version 2.9.3 or higher.

# Prerequiste: Make sure you have installed a linux distro
wsl --list --online
wsl --install -d Ubuntu

# Install WSL 
wsl --update --pre-release
wslc version

# Test that everything works correctly by running a simple built-in image.
wslc run --rm hello-world
# You should see a "Hello" message confirming that your installation appears to be working correctly.

##  Run your first containers
./run-container.ps1

# Because the nginx container was started with --rm, it is removed automatically once it stops.

## Use vscode to develop apps with wslc.exe
# Install wsl extension on vscode

# Let's build a container image for an existing app project.
# To download the HelloWorld-Django web app from GitHub, open a WSL terminal (Ubuntu for example)
wsl -d Ubuntu
git clone https://github.com/Promise-Igbo/helloworld-django.git

# Always store your code in the same file system that you're using tools in. This will result in faster file access performance.
# store your project files on the WSL file system \\wsl$.

# From your WSL terminal, change directories to the source code folder for this project:
cd helloworld-django

# Open the project in VS Code running on the local WSL extension server by entering:
code .

# Confirm that you are connected to your WSL Linux distro by checking the green remote indicator in the bottom-left corner of your VS Code instance.

# Add a Containerfile to the root of your project that describes how to build the image.
wslc build -t helloworld-django .

# Confirm that the image was created by listing your images
wslc image list

# Run your newly built image as a container, publishing the app's port so you can reach it from Windows:
wslc run -d --rm -p 8000:8000 --name django helloworld-django

# list the running containers and check its logs
wslc container list
wslc container logs django

# Open a browser on Windows and navigate to http://localhost:8000/ t

## Things to note
# Inspect a container or image
wslc container inspect <container-id>
wslc container logs <container-id>
wslc image inspect <image>

# Reclaim disk space
# Remove all stopped containers
wslc container prune

# Remove unused images
wslc image pruneo see your app running inside its container. 

# Or, confirm that the app is running on Linux by executing a command inside the container
wslc exec django uname

# The uname result of "Linux" confirms that the container is running on the WSL 2 Linux kernel

# stop the container
wslc container stop django


